{
 "id": "come-thou-fount",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": false,
 "title": "Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing",
 "description": "Robert Robinson's hymn of 1758 to the American tune Nettleton, in the four parts The Methodist Hymnal printed in 1905, for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse, bright and lilting in three, every line one breath. The alto sings close under the tune, and for \"Teach me some melodious sonnet\" everyone climbs together before the opening tune comes back.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "hymn"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 90,
 "key": "Eb",
 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Robert Robinson (1758)",
  "tune": "\"Nettleton\", from Wyeth's Repository of Sacred Music, Part Second (1813)",
  "arrangement": "The Methodist Hymnal (1905), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Robinson died 1790",
  "tune": "public domain: printed 1813",
  "arrangement": "the four parts for Nettleton as printed, unsigned, in The Methodist Hymnal (public domain: published in the United States in 1905), in its key: its tune, alto, tenor and bass, with three bass notes changed so the bass does not leap a minor sixth mid-line, one two-note tenor syllable sung on one note, and six alto notes changed so the duet sings thirds through \"to sing thy\" and closes the second line and the verse on a full chord; the print's dashes in the seventh line written as commas; breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "The Methodist Hymnal: Official Hymnal of the Methodist Episcopal Church and the Methodist Episcopal Church, South (New York: Eaton & Mains; Cincinnati: Jennings & Graham, 1905), No. 19, \"Nettleton\", p. 14, the tune credited to John Wyeth and the harmony unsigned: its four parts in E-flat, verse 1 as printed under the music, the Amen left out. Princeton Theological Seminary Library copy, archive.org/details/methodisthymnalo00meth/page/n27. Checked against The Popular Hymnal, ed. Robert H. Coleman (Dallas: Robert H. Coleman, 1918), No. 36, \"Come, Thou Fount\", which prints the same four parts (University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Music Library copy, archive.org/details/popularhymnalold00cole/page/n29), and against The Hymnal (Philadelphia: Presbyterian Board of Publication, 1895; 1906 printing), No. 589, second tune, which has the same tune and the same first, second and last lines' harmony but sets the middle lines over a held bass (archive.org/details/psalter00pres/page/n497); the tune compared with Wyeth's \"Hallelujah\" as Allen D. Carden reprinted it in The Missouri Harmony (St. Louis, 1820), p. 72 (archive.org/details/missouriharmonyo00card_0/page/n87)"
 },
